# Context and requirements

The requirements part of an Effect type names the services that the work needs.
Sam describes this requirements channel as a way to model permissions before execution, which he calls a static effect.
He connects that idea to Alchemy inferring which identity and access management (IAM) policy a program needs. [Happy Path 116, 11:54](https://youtu.be/YEn4A7XxgF4?t=714).

Michael describes an Effect by its output, predictable failures, and requirements. [Happy Path 81, 49:15](https://youtu.be/gc5P36t6kD0?t=2955).

See [Effect basics](/lore/effect-basics) and [infrastructure as effects](/lore/infrastructure-as-effects).

Read [why dependency injection matters](/lore/dependency-injection-is-the-reason-to-choose-effect).
